sual incumbency advantage", "Nearly all expert raters call the race Lean D or better, with markets pricing Sand near 87-89%", "Iowa's Republican-leaning baseline partially offsets the polling edge"], "rationale": "Every recent poll, including Republican-sponsored surveys, shows Sand ahead by four to twelve points, and raters and prediction markets converge on a clear Democratic edge in this open-seat race. The state's Republican lean and normal late-cycle uncertainty temper the polling average, leaving Sand a solid but not safe favorite.", "watch": "A late break toward Lahn after the debate attacks on taxes and residency, or polling error in a state Republicans have recently dominated, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open this year with Kim Reynolds retiring, and State Auditor Rob Sand has emerged as a strong Democratic contender against Republican Zach Lahn. Polling since early September consistently shows Sand ahead, with the most recent surveys from SSRS, Suffolk, and the New York Times/Siena giving him leads of nine to twelve points. Even Republican-sponsored polls show him in front, though by smaller margins.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly call the race Lean D, and prediction markets put Sand's chances near 87 to 89 percent. The first debate featured sharp clashes over taxes and residency, and Lahn's attacks on Sand's tax record are being fact-checked. Iowa's Republican fundamentals keep this from being a safe bet, but the weight of evidence points to a Democratic flip.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8472, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8297, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem"